WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:04.850
The ongoing nature of television series means
they tend to spawn more fan theories than

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.490
any other form of entertainment.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.780
Amid the ones that are absolutely ridiculous,
are some that are actually kind of plausible.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.740
Let's dive down the rabbit hole and look at
some crazy fan theories that totally change

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.160
these TV shows.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.400
Bikini Bottom: nuclear wasteland

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.190
In one popular fan theory, Spongebob Squarepants,
Squidward, Patrick, and all the rest of Bikini

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:28.330
Bottom's citizens were irradiated during atomic
tests, turning them into the bizarre characters

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:29.330
we see today.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:38.430
“I don’t know what do you wanna do today?”

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.580
But how'd that even happen?

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.630
Well, the fictional Bikini Bottom is located
on the ocean floor under a place called Bikini

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:44.630
Atoll.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:49.060
That name may sound familiar, because Bikini
Atoll was famously the site of 23 atomic bomb

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.489
tests in the '40s and '50s … including some
underwater detonations.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:58.040
These explosions left the island and reef
exposed to massive amounts of radioactive

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.320
fallout, and Bikini Atoll remains uninhabitable
to this day.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.320
Unless you live in a pineapple.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.970
“Ohhhh, who lives in a pineapple under the
sea.”

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.810
“SpongeBob squarepants.”

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.760
The Paddy's Pub gang is way uglier than you
think

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.610
This awesome theory about It's Always Sunny
in Philadelphia comes to us courtesy of a

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.970
Reddit thread from 2015.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:21.180
In it, a user going by the name CraigsBenedict
proposes that Mac, Dee, and Dennis are not

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.700
really as attractive as they appear to us
viewers.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.671
The idea relies on the "unreliable narrator"
premise, in which the story is told from the

00:01:27.671 --> 00:01:31.680
characters' narcissistic perspectives, and
is not how things actually are.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.650
“Alright, but you get my point your honor
and you can see that Mack was slowing down

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.330
at the end there and let the record show”

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.320
“Because he only works out his glamour muscles.”

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:41.330
“No, that’s, that’s bullsh*t.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.070
I did like 3 more than you.”

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.640
There's also some evidence to back this idea
up: in the episode "High School Reunion Part

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.830
2," the gang choreographs an amazing dance
routine to impress all the jerks who teased

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:51.970
them in high school.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.200
“Freedom, Freedom”

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.740
Of course, what they're actually doing is
this:

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.380
“Ahhhhhhhhhh”

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.230
If we take this theory seriously, it could
explain why the rest of the world treats these

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.240
three attractive people like total freaks.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.590
Well, except for the fact that they routinely
act like total freaks.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.880
“So, you guys ever eat cheese.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.900
Cheese is a funny thing, cheese is a strange
thing.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.410
I always wonder where what is cheese where
does ahhhhhhh.”

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.260
Professor Samurai Jack

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.560
In 1998, animator Genndy Tartakovsky was a
supervising producer on The Powerpuff Girls

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.700
for the Cartoon Network.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.780
It follows the adventures of three superpowered
sisters, who were created in a lab by a guy

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.080
named Professor Utonium:

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.840
“I just wouldn’t want to touch it, yuck.”

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.780
“Actually, girls, to him, well, you are
pretty gross yourselves.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.610
With your big heads, large eyes, little bodies.”

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.000
“Ok”

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.180
Then, in 2001, Tartakovsky created another
show called Samurai Jack, all about a warrior

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.629
ripped from the past and thrown into the future,
who wages battle against an immortal demon

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:53.629
named Aku:

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:56.760
“I am the last son of the land that you
have pillaged.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:03.319
I am here to reclaim it, for my people, for
my father, for my birthright.”

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:07.200
Throughout the series, Samurai Jack wanders
around a dystopian, post-apocalyptic world.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.680
And at one point, he makes his way to a ruined
city that looks kind of familiar...because

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.980
it sure seems to resemble the city of Townsville
from The Powerpuff Girls.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.040
Not only that, but did you ever notice how
Jack kind looks a whole lot like the Professor?

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.459
Just what went wrong in Townsville?

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:22.079
Is this Mojo Jojo's fault?

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.440
“Haaaaaahaaaaaaa”

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.069
Ash is forever young because of a magical
Pokémon

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:34.819
Why is Ash Ketchum, the hero of multiple Pokemon
animated series, always ten years old?

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:38.170
Through 19 seasons and 18 movies, he never
ages.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:41.700
Time definitely passes on the shows, because
Ash spends weeks trekking through the various

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.849
regions on his quest to collect badges and
become a Pokémon master, so by all accounts,

00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:47.959
he should be at least a teenager by now.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:51.940
The answer may have come in the very first
episode of the original series, when Ash witnesses

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.800
a beautiful bird Pokémon flying into a rainbow.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.040
"What's that?”

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:01.960
“There is no data.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.180
There are still pokemon yet to be identified.”

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.239
We later find out that this was a legendary
Pokémon called Ho-oh.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:11.489
The games say that anyone who sees Ho-oh is
promised eternal happiness.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:15.940
And since Ash's fondest wish is to forever
travel the world collecting and training Pokémon,

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.969
the Ho-oh granted this wish, giving Ash eternal
youth.

00:04:18.969 --> 00:04:21.489
Sherlock Holmes and Spock are related

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:25.121
This is probably the best theory on the list,
since it might not simply be a theory, at

00:04:25.121 --> 00:04:27.169
least in the eyes of Star Trek fans everywhere.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.490
In Star Trek VI: The Undiscovered Country,
Spock — who, we must remind you, is half-human

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:35.800
— must solve a mystery in space...and to
do so, he relies on not only his Vulcan logic,

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.869
but his human ancestry, too:

00:04:37.869 --> 00:04:42.119
“An ancestor of mine maintained that if
you eliminate the impossible that whatever

00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:45.270
remains, however improbable must be the truth.”

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.849
And that's not the only time Spock relies
on the wisdom of his forebears, either:

00:04:48.849 --> 00:04:51.080
“Are you actually suggesting they're from the future.”

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:54.979
“If you eliminate the impossible whatever
remains, however improbable must be the truth.”

00:04:54.979 --> 00:04:55.979
“How poetic.”

00:04:55.979 --> 00:04:59.740
If that quote's a little familiar, you may
be a fan of Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's Sherlock

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.740
Holmes novels.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.819
The legendary detective first uttered the
quote himself in the 1890 story "The Sign

00:05:04.819 --> 00:05:05.819
of the Four."

00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:06.819
It gets crazier.

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:10.900
In the modern BBC series Sherlock, Benedict
Cumberbatch has said the line himself as well:

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.849
“Once you rule out the impossible whatever
remains, however improbable must be true.”

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:18.660
And then Sherlock and Spock finally met in
2013...kind of:

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.479
“You are suggesting the admiral violated
every regulation he vowed to uphold simply

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:25.479
because he wanted to exploit your intellect.”

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:27.259
“He wanted to exploit my savagery.

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:30.479
My name is Kaan.”

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.020
“The fly in the ointment.”

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:37.820
“Well, alright Spock just take it easy.”

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:38.420
Ah well.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:42.399
Maybe someday we'll get the Sherlock/Star
Trek crossover we've all been hoping for.

00:05:42.399 --> 00:05:43.449
Oh, wait.

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:48.960
“There can be no argument, the game is afoot.”

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:49.960
Never mind!

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:50.960
Thanks for watching!

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.629
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:53.629 --> 00:05:57.169
And leave us a comment to let us know which
fan theories we should've added to the list...

